# axRange Proxmox adapter configuration
# Copy to /etc/axrange/adapter.conf and fill in the operator-side values.
# Env vars (PROXMOX_API_URL, OVS_SSH_KEY, etc.) override this file.

# The deploy platform this file configures: selects the adapter (and the
# image bindings under images/bindings/<platform>/) for every command that
# loads it. `proxmox` when absent; a target's `platform:` in the targets
# file must agree with it. See adapter.conf.libvirt.example for the other.
PLATFORM=proxmox

# --- REST backend (Proxmox API) ---
PROXMOX_API_URL=https://10.0.0.85:8006/api2/json
# PVEAPIToken=<user>@<realm>!<token-id>=<secret>
PROXMOX_API_TOKEN=PVEAPIToken=axr@pve!planner=REPLACE-WITH-TOKEN-SECRET
# Set false for self-signed cluster certs (slice default; tighten in prod).
PROXMOX_VERIFY_TLS=false

# --- SSH backend (ovs-vsctl + hook install) ---
OVS_SSH_HOST=10.0.0.85
OVS_SSH_USER=ovs-operator
OVS_SSH_KEY=~/.ssh/pxtwin1_ed25519

# --- Guest cloud-init (optional) ---
# SSH public key axrange injects into deployed VMs so the operator
# can `ssh <ciuser>@<vm-ip>` after deploy. Leave empty to skip key
# injection; `qm guest exec` from the Proxmox node still works.
GUEST_SSH_PUBKEY_FILE=~/.ssh/pxtwin1_ed25519.pub

# --- Cluster facts the planner needs ---
# Default Proxmox node name (slice = single-node placement).
DEFAULT_NODE=pxtwin1

# --- Deploy-wide default password (docs/WORK_ORDER_access_facts.md Part A) ---
# Fills every node/account login that has no password of its own (console +
# SSH-by-password on Linux; Administrator/safemode/domain-admin on Windows).
# An explicitly-set password (a binding's cloud_init password, a per-identity
# IdentityEntry.password, an intentionally-weak AD account) still wins.
# Unset keeps the historical lab-default constant shown below.
# SECURITY: setting this (or even leaving it at the built-in default) turns ON
# SSH password login lab-wide on every Linux node, with a well-known password --
# understand the posture change before using this outside an isolated lab.
# DEFAULT_PASSWORD=Axr4nge-Lab!

# Sensor-agnostic te (2026-07-13, docs/WORK_ORDER_sensor_agnostic_te.md): no
# SENSOR_TRANSPORT_IP / SENSOR_<ID>_TRANSPORT_IP / SENSOR_SSH_* /
# SENSOR_CAPTURE_IFACE keys any more. A `te_` deploy wires NO mirror/gretap/
# heartbeat of its own — it records tap-INTENT only (OP-MIR-005
# record-tap-intent). The sensor's transport IP + vmid are discovered from
# A1's standing O2 inventory by the A2 tap playout (`axrange tap deploy`)
# at tap-bind time, never read from this file.

# --- O2 surface (observability endpoints; consumed by `axrange o2 info`,
# docs/WORK_ORDER_cli_lifecycle.md §3.4) ---
# These live on the dedicated O2 server-stack VM, not in the deploy state
# store, so this file is the one authoritative source. All optional; an
# unconfigured endpoint is unknown, never derived from a guessed sensor IP.
# O2_GRAFANA_URL=http://10.0.0.90:3000
# O2_LOKI_URL=http://10.0.0.90:3100
# O2_PROMETHEUS_URL=http://10.0.0.90:9090
# O2_CALDERA_URL=http://10.0.0.90:8888
# O2_WAZUH_URL=https://10.0.0.90:443

# --- O2 services VM register + auto-resolve (docs/WORK_ORDER_access_facts.md
# Part B) --- an alternative to hand-copying every O2_*_URL above: declare the
# server-stack VM (vmid and/or name; vmid is preferred/faster) and `o2 info`
# resolves its live IP over the Proxmox API and derives the five endpoint URLs
# itself. A per-service O2_*_URL above still WINS over this for that service.
# O2_SERVICES_VMID=102
# O2_SERVICES_NAME=o2-services
# Optional per-service port overrides; defaults shown (wazuh is https, the
# rest are http).
# O2_GRAFANA_PORT=3000
# O2_LOKI_PORT=3100
# O2_PROMETHEUS_PORT=9090
# O2_CALDERA_PORT=8888
# O2_WAZUH_PORT=443

# Network sensor (OP-OVL deploy-network-sensor, Option C — docs/SLICE_OVERLAY.md
# §4b). Standing Suricata network-sensor VM fed a mirror of the capture appliance's
# capture iface; the defense overlay configures it per-scenario. Resolved at
# deploy (plan is network-sensor-agnostic).
# Leave unset unless a scenario declares a network sensor (overlays.defense observes:).
# NETWORK_SENSOR_VMID=8001
# NETWORK_SENSOR_IFACE=cap-mirror

# --- In-scenario sensor capture retention (optional; tcpdump-sensor's
# in-guest disk-%/file-count prune watchdog, config_exec/render.py) ---
# Prune oldest pcaps once the capture filesystem's used% reaches HIGH, down
# to below LOW (user decision 2026-07-17). Defaults 85/70 if unset (LOW is
# clamped below HIGH if misconfigured).
# CAPTURE_HIGH_WATER_PCT=85
# CAPTURE_LOW_WATER_PCT=70
# Independent of disk%: prune oldest once the pcap file count exceeds this
# (catches idle-link inode growth from many tiny rotated files). Defaults
# 2000 if unset.
# CAPTURE_MAX_FILES=2000

# --- Mirror gretap queueing (optional) ---
# `attach-gre-tunnel` replaces the node-default qdisc on each mirror gretap
# with `pfifo limit N` and sets txqueuelen N.
#
# Why: a new `ip link ... type gretap` inherits the NODE's default qdisc,
# which on PVE 9.x is fq_codel -- an AQM whose job is to DROP packets to hold
# latency down. That is correct for a congested uplink and exactly wrong for a
# capture path: the mirrored copy is fire-and-forget, so a drop is lost
# evidence and there is no sender to signal. Measured on dev45 2026-08-13:
# 9,349 frames lost in a 90s window at ~2.38 Gbit/s with fq_codel, zero after
# this change -- worth roughly a 60% increase in lossless mirror throughput.
#
# Asserted on EVERY run, not just on create, so a gretap built before this
# change heals on the next `tap deploy` instead of keeping fq_codel forever.
#
# Set 0 to leave the node default alone (opt-out for a node whose qdisc is
# managed elsewhere). Defaults 20000 if unset.
# MIRROR_GRETAP_TXQUEUELEN=20000

# --- Protected external nodes (optional; blast-radius guard) ---
# Comma-separated allowlist of operator-managed nodes created BY HAND, outside
# axrange's deploy lifecycle entirely, that no teardown (any tier -- te/tap/o2)
# may ever destroy -- on top of the built-in default (XPress-SW, the
# packetXpress sink/capture box). Unconditional, like a golden template: even
# an `axrange o2 destroy` refuses these.
# PROTECTED_EXTERNAL_NAMES=XPress-SW
# Same idea, by vmid -- a review-hardening belt: a teardown refuses these
# vmids even if the PVE display-name lookup fails to resolve (the fail-open
# gap the name guard alone can't close). Auto-unioned with every
# EXTERNAL_SENSORS[].vmid below -- you don't need to also list it here.
# PROTECTED_EXTERNAL_VMIDS=250

# --- Config-declared EXTERNAL sensors (optional) ---
# A JSON list of operator-provisioned sensor nodes a tap plan may direct
# traffic to WITHOUT them ever appearing in A1's standing O2 inventory --
# e.g. the packetXpress XPress-SW sink/capture box. axRange NEVER provisions
# or reaches into one of these: it only points a gretap's `remote` at
# transport_ip. Declaring one here is SUFFICIENT to protect it from every
# tier's teardown (id -> PROTECTED_EXTERNAL_NAMES, vmid ->
# PROTECTED_EXTERNAL_VMIDS, both automatic) -- no double-declaration needed.
#
# Required manual steps on the XPress-SW side (operator-owned, once):
#   1. Give it a transport NIC on the same isolated transport vnet/subnet
#      axRange's tap playout uses, with the static IP you put in
#      transport_ip below.
#   2. `ip link add gre-cap type gretap external` (a FLOW-based gretap --
#      accepts any GRE key, so it merges every tapped bridge's traffic that
#      targets this sensor; matches capture_iface below).
#   3. Run whatever capture tool you want against `gre-cap` (tcpdump,
#      Suricata, the packetXpress agent, ...) -- axRange has no opinion and
#      does not manage it.
#
# EXTERNAL_SENSORS=[{"id":"XPress-SW","vmid":250,"transport_ip":"10.255.250.20","type":"packetxpress","capture_iface":"gre-cap"}]
#
# A hand-built tcpdump sensor can be declared here the SAME way, instead of
# letting `axrange o2 deploy` synthesize one (deliberate, per-cluster choice):
# build the VM by hand with its capture dir on a virtio-fs share mapped from a
# Proxmox-host directory (so pcaps are host-readable AND survive VM rebuilds),
# then declare it below. scripts/o2-pcaps-{list,fetch}.sh read that host dir.
# Naming convention: <Type>-SW-<Letter> (mirrors XPress-SW / XPress-SW-A) — the
# trailing letter is a per-instance tag, so a lab grows Tcpdump-SW-A, -B, …; keep
# the adapter.conf id, the PVE VM name, and the host share subdir identical.
# The entries coexist in one JSON list:
# EXTERNAL_SENSORS=[{"id":"XPress-SW","vmid":250,"transport_ip":"10.255.250.20","type":"packetxpress","capture_iface":"gre-cap"},{"id":"Tcpdump-SW-A","vmid":260,"transport_ip":"10.255.250.11","type":"tcpdump","capture_iface":"gre-cap"}]

# --- Image build pipeline (axrange image build) ---
# docs/IMAGE_BUILD.md §5.2: building needs a BROADER credential scope than the
# deploy-time token (VM create / disk import / VM destroy / template convert).
# Every BUILD_* key is OPTIONAL and falls back to its deploy-scope counterpart,
# so a single-account lab can leave this whole section commented out.
#
# Broader REST token for building (falls back to PROXMOX_API_TOKEN):
# BUILD_API_TOKEN=PVEAPIToken=axr@pve!imagebuild=REPLACE-WITH-TOKEN-SECRET
# SSH identity with `qm` rights — root or a build-operator (falls back to OVS_SSH_*):
# BUILD_SSH_USER=root
# BUILD_SSH_KEY=~/.ssh/pxtwin1_ed25519
# BUILD_SSH_HOST=10.0.0.85
# Build target node (falls back to DEFAULT_NODE):
# BUILD_NODE=pxtwin1
# Storage for the build VM disk + resulting template:
# BUILD_STORAGE=local-lvm
# Bridge with internet access for the bake (apt installs qemu-guest-agent):
# BUILD_BRIDGE=vmbr0
# Storage that holds cloud-init snippets (must support snippets):
# BUILD_SNIPPET_STORAGE=local
# Local cache dir for downloaded upstream cloud images:
# BUILD_CACHE_DIR=~/.axrange/cache

